                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                        "login": "echasnovski",
                        "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                        "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> After this, if I run `vim.api.nvim_input' n'` it does wait for next input and works like a submode. But if I run `vim.api.nvim_input' nn'` it moves down once, but doesn't stay in submode.\r\n\r\nThat's probably related to the fact that triggers are implemented through buffer-local mappings. So as there is a buffer-local mapping `<Space>n`, using `nvim_input` with it as input works. However, in the same situation, two consecutive calls like `nvim_input(' n')` and `nvim_input('n')` don't work (even with `vim.loop.sleep(1)` in the middle.\r\n\r\nHonestly, this is a territory of studying by trial and error about the exact behavior Neovim processes key presses. And this logic mostly comes from Vim, so might be (and often is) unintuitive. This was the experience during 'mini.clue' development and I am glad it is at least in the state that works interactively :sweat_smile: ",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">After this, if I run <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.api.nvim_input' n'</code> it does wait for next input and works like a submode. But if I run <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.api.nvim_input' nn'</code> it moves down once, but doesn't stay in submode.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">That's probably related to the fact that triggers are implemented through buffer-local mappings. So as there is a buffer-local mapping <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;Space&gt;n</code>, using <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim_input</code> with it as input works. However, in the same situation, two consecutive calls like <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim_input(' n')</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim_input('n')</code> don't work (even with <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.loop.sleep(1)</code> in the middle.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Honestly, this is a territory of studying by trial and error about the exact behavior Neovim processes key presses. And this logic mostly comes from Vim, so might be (and often is) unintuitive. This was the experience during 'mini.clue' development and I am glad it is at least in the state that works interactively 😅</p>",
                    "bodyText": "After this, if I run vim.api.nvim_input' n' it does wait for next input and works like a submode. But if I run vim.api.nvim_input' nn' it moves down once, but doesn't stay in submode.\n\nThat's probably related to the fact that triggers are implemented through buffer-local mappings. So as there is a buffer-local mapping <Space>n, using nvim_input with it as input works. However, in the same situation, two consecutive calls like nvim_input(' n') and nvim_input('n') don't work (even with vim.loop.sleep(1) in the middle.\nHonestly, this is a territory of studying by trial and error about the exact behavior Neovim processes key presses. And this logic mostly comes from Vim, so might be (and often is) unintuitive. This was the experience during 'mini.clue' development and I am glad it is at least in the state that works interactively 😅",
